One common way this occurs is through **Path Traversal** vulnerabilities, where an application takes user input to construct a file path without proper validation. This allows an attacker to access sensitive files on the server (like `/etc/passwd`) by using `../` sequences.

```java
import java.io.*;
import java.nio.file.*;

public class FileServer {
    public void readFile(String filename) throws IOException {
        // This code is vulnerable: the filename is concatenated directly 
        // into the path, allowing directory traversal attacks.
        File file = new File("C:/app/data/" + filename);
        byte[] content = Files.readAllBytes(file.toPath());
        System.out.println(new String(content));
    }
}
***
import java.io.*;
import java.nio.file.*;
import java.io.IOException;

public class FileServer {
    public void readFile(String filename) throws IOException {
        Path baseDirectory = Paths.get("C:/app/data/").toAbsolutePath().normalize();

        // Resolve the filename against the base directory and normalize to remove "../"
        Path targetPath = baseDirectory.resolve(filename).normalize();

        // Security check: Ensure the resulting path still resides within the base directory
        if (!targetPath.startsWith(baseDirectory)) {
            throw new SecurityException("Access Denied: Invalid file path provided.");
        }

        byte[] content = Files.readAllBytes(targetPath);
        System.out.println(new String(content));
    }
}
```

